<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lecale</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lecale is a fertile lowland district of east County Down, Northern Ireland, between Strangford Lough, Dundrum Bay and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/downpatrick">Downpatrick</a>. It was the heartland of the Dál Fiatach kings of the Ulaid and later of the Anglo-Norman earldom of Ulster, whose castles and tower houses still stand across the area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lecale.jpg" alt="Lecale" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mabuska · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lecale.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lecale, from the Irish Leath Cathail, Cathal's half, is a peninsula bounded by Strangford Lough to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> to the east and Dundrum inner bay to the west, with St. John's point at its southern tip. Its older name, Magh Inis, the island plain, and the phrase Isle Lecale recall that the sea almost encircled it until Edward Southwell built the first sea barriers in 1745, which created the River Quoile and 500 acres of land around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/downpatrick">Downpatrick</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Leath Cathail took its name from Cathal, a prince of the Ulaid about 700 and a descendant of Fiachna, son of Deaman, a king of the Ulaid: it was Cathal's half of Dál Fiatach. The Cenél nÓengusa are recorded as its kings and the Ua Flathraoi as its lords by the 12th century. According to the Annals of the Four Masters, on 29 June 1147 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-neogain">Cenél nEógain</a> defeated the Ulaid at Dundrum Bay, killing Árchú Ua Flathraoi, lord of Leath-Chathail, plundering Lecale and carrying off hostages.</p><p>Saint Patrick landed at the Slaney estuary on his return to Ireland, died at the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a> and was carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/downpatrick">Downpatrick</a>. The relics of Brigid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a> were brought there in 835 and those of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> in 877; rediscovered in 1185, they were placed in a tomb in the abbey on 9 June 1186 at the wish of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-de-courcy">John de Courcy</a>, who is said to have built Dundrum Castle for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>. In 1226 Lecale, as Ladcathel, was one of the five counties of the Earldom of Ulster, and by 1333 it had been replaced by Down.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitzgeralds-of-kildare">Earls of Kildare</a> later held the customs of Strangford and Ardglass, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> granted the port's revenue of £5,000 to Gerald Fitzgerald, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-kildare">Earl of Kildare</a>. Thomas Russell, a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a> in the rising of 1803, was hanged at the gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/downpatrick">Downpatrick</a> gaol on 21 October 1803.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lecale became a barony, split into Lecale Lower and Lecale Upper by 1851. Its largest settlement is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/downpatrick">Downpatrick</a>, with Ardglass, Killough and Strangford among the others, and it has a high concentration of tower houses. In 1967 an area of 3,108 hectares between the Mourne Mountains and Strangford Lough was designated the Lecale Coast Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lecale” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their wealth lay in the fertile lands of <b>Lecale</b> and the Ards, and the monasteries of Bangor and Movilla lay nearby.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-fiatach">Dal Fiatach</a></li></ul></section>
